    <h2>What this page could not verify<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <div class="warn">
      Four things, stated plainly so you know which parts of this page are reads and which are reconstruction.
      <br><br>
      <b>1. How the dartboard paper produces 7561.</b> The four lines read <b>1+6</b>, <b>4/6</b>, <b>2-5</b> and <b>3-1</b>, and those reads held up at two zoom levels. But no frame in the recording shows the paper being used, and no frame shows a working being written down. The code <b>7561</b> itself is solid — it was read twice off the keypad display — but the arithmetic that gets you there is not on film, so this page does not invent one.
      <br><br>
      <b>2. The final letters on the three-dial machine.</b> The drums were still moving when the compartment opened. The last clean read was <b>T Y N</b> at 7:25, and a 4x crop of the next second read the third box as an <b>L</b>; the Key appears at 7:26 either way. With three scrolling drums and no solved frame, this page prints the reading rather than a "correct" word.
      <br><br>
      <b>3. What the Token physically goes into.</b> The control panel with the two red squares and two empty slots is filmed at 2:43, the Token is picked up at 2:47, and the Breakout game is running by 3:08. That sequence is consistent with dropping the Token into a slot, but the drop is never shown, so it stays an inference.
      <br><br>
      <b>4. The payoffs of the record display and the arrow board.</b> Both open, both are worked, both end cleanly — the records sparkle at 7:31 — but neither shows a readable solved state or a reward we could tie to a later lock. They are described here, not solved.
    </div>
